      body:
        "**John Locke** (1632–1704), an English philosopher, argued in *Two Treatises of Government* (published 1689) that everyone has **natural rights** — to **life, liberty and property** — which exist before any government. People create governments **by consent** to protect those rights; a government that breaks that trust can rightfully be resisted and replaced. French Enlightenment writers added more ideas, such as Montesquieu's **separation of powers**.\n\n| Date | Document | Key idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 July 1776 | US Declaration of Independence (drafted mainly by Thomas Jefferson) | 'All men are created equal'; governments get their powers from 'the consent of the governed' |\n| 1787–91 | US Constitution and Bill of Rights (the first ten amendments, 1791) | Elected President and Congress; separation of powers; freedom of speech and religion; fair trials |\n| 26 Aug 1789 | French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen | 'Men are born and remain free and equal in rights'; power comes from the nation |\n\n**The contradiction.** These documents promised universal rights, but many people were excluded. Slavery continued in the United States until 1865. Women were left out: in 1791 **Olympe de Gouges** wrote a *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen*; she was executed in 1793. Enslaved people forced the issue themselves: the **Haitian Revolution** (1791–1804) created the first state founded by formerly enslaved people.\n\n**Abolition in Britain.** Campaigners such as Thomas Clarkson, William Wilberforce and **Olaudah Equiano**, a formerly enslaved writer, built a mass movement of petitions and sugar boycotts, while revolts by enslaved people, such as in Jamaica in 1831–32, raised the pressure. Parliament abolished the **slave trade in 1807** and **slavery in most of the British Empire in 1833** (in force from 1834). The government paid £20 million in compensation — to slave owners, not to the people who had been enslaved — and freed people were forced into unpaid 'apprenticeships' until 1838.",
