      id: "human-rights-after-1945",
      heading: "Human rights after 1945",
      discovery: {
        problem:
          "It is 1946. The Second World War is over. Hitler had been appointed legally, after his party won more seats than any other in elections, and his regime then used laws to strip Jewish people and others of their rights, step by step, before the Holocaust.\n\nYou sit on a new world committee. **Draft three rights** that every person should have, whoever and wherever they are. Then face the hard question: if a government breaks them, **who will enforce them?**",
        idea:
          "The committee's answer was the **Universal Declaration of Human Rights** (1948): rights belong to every human being simply for being human — they are not a gift from a government, and a majority cannot vote them away. That was the lesson of the 1930s: elections alone do not make a free society.\n\nThe weakness is the one you probably spotted: the UDHR was a **declaration**, not a law, so there was no court to enforce it. Its power came from the standard it set, which campaigners and later treaties used.",
      },
      body:
        "**Lessons of war.** The Second World War (1939–45) and the **Holocaust** — the Nazi murder of about six million Jews, alongside the persecution and murder of Roma and Sinti people, disabled people and others — showed that a modern state could remove every right by law. At the **Nuremberg trials** (1945–46) leading Nazis were tried for 'crimes against humanity'.\n\n**The United Nations** was founded on **24 October 1945** to keep the peace and promote human rights. Its Commission on Human Rights was chaired by **Eleanor Roosevelt**, widow of US President Franklin D. Roosevelt. The drafters came from many countries — including René Cassin (France), Peng Chun Chang (China), Charles Malik (Lebanon) and Hansa Mehta (India), who is credited with making sure it said 'all human beings' rather than 'all men'.\n\nThe **Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR)** was adopted by the UN General Assembly in Paris on **10 December 1948**: 48 votes for, none against, 8 abstentions. Its 30 articles include:\n\n- Article 1 — all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights\n- Article 5 — no torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment\n- Article 10 — a fair and public hearing by an independent court\n- Article 21 — the right to take part in government, through genuine elections by universal and equal suffrage and secret vote\n\n**From words to reality.** The UDHR was not legally binding, and many countries kept colonies or racial segregation. But campaigners used its language. In the USA, the **civil rights movement** — Rosa Parks and the Montgomery bus boycott (1955–56), Martin Luther King's 'I have a dream' speech at the March on Washington (1963) — won the Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965). In **South Africa**, the system of racial segregation called **apartheid** ended with the first elections open to all races in **1994**, when Nelson Mandela became president.\n\n> “All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights.” — UDHR, Article 1, 1948",
      diagram: `<svg viewBox="0 0 480 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Map of rights in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights of 10 December 1948: equality and no discrimination, no slavery or torture, fair trial, freedom of thought and expression, taking part in government through free elections, and the rights to work, equal pay and education"><text x="240" y="18" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif" fill="#1f2937">The UDHR: some of its 30 articles</text><g stroke="#334155" stroke-width="1.5"><line x1="160" y1="58" x2="185" y2="120"/><line x1="320" y1="58" x2="295" y2="120"/><line x1="160" y1="150" x2="168" y2="150"/><line x1="320" y1="150" x2="312" y2="150"/><line x1="160" y1="242" x2="185" y2="180"/><line x1="320" y1="242" x2="295" y2="180"/></g><ellipse cx="240" cy="150" rx="72" ry="42" fill="#bae6fd" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2"/><g stroke="#334155"><rect x="10" y="30" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#c7d2fe"/><rect x="320" y="30" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#fecaca"/><rect x="10" y="122" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#fde68a"/><rect x="320" y="122" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#c7d2fe"/><rect x="10" y="214" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#c7d2fe"/><rect x="320" y="214" width="150" height="56" rx="6" fill="#bbf7d0"/></g><g font-family="sans-serif" fill="#1f2937" text-anchor="middle"><text x="240" y="144" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">UDHR</text><text x="240" y="160" font-size="11">10 Dec 1948</text><text x="240" y="174" font-size="11">30 articles</text><text x="85" y="48" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Articles 1–2</text><text x="85" y="63" font-size="10">Born free and equal;</text><text x="85" y="77" font-size="10">no discrimination</text><text x="395" y="48" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Articles 4–5</text><text x="395" y="63" font-size="10">No slavery; no torture</text><text x="395" y="77" font-size="10">or cruel punishment</text><text x="85" y="140" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Articles 9–11</text><text x="85" y="155" font-size="10">No arbitrary arrest;</text><text x="85" y="169" font-size="10">fair, public trial</text><text x="395" y="140" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Articles 18–19</text><text x="395" y="155" font-size="10">Freedom of thought,</text><text x="395" y="169" font-size="10">religion and expression</text><text x="85" y="232" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Article 21</text><text x="85" y="247" font-size="10">Take part in government;</text><text x="85" y="261" font-size="10">free, secret elections</text><text x="395" y="232" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">Articles 23 and 26</text><text x="395" y="247" font-size="10">Work, equal pay for</text><text x="395" y="261" font-size="10">equal work; education</text><text x="240" y="292" font-size="10">Not a law in 1948 — but a shared standard that later treaties and campaigners used</text></g></svg>`,
