      heading: "Democracy in Singapore",
      discovery: {
        problem:
          "You probably know these words:\n\n> “We, the citizens of Singapore, pledge ourselves as one united people, regardless of race, language or religion, to build a democratic society based on justice and equality…” — the National Pledge, written by S. Rajaratnam, 1966\n\nNow look at the number of registered voters in Singapore elections (rounded):\n\n| 1948 | 1955 | 1959 |\n|---|---|---|\n| about 23,000 | about 300,000 | about 590,000 |\n\nThe electorate grew about 25 times in eleven years. Before reading on, suggest **two changes** that could explain such a jump.",
        idea:
          "Two changes did most of the work: **automatic registration** of voters (1955), and a **citizenship law** (1957) that let many people born in China, India, Malaya and elsewhere become Singapore citizens. A third change, **compulsory voting** (1959), made sure the people on the register actually voted, so turnout rose sharply.\n\nJust as in Britain, representation widened by changing the rules about who counts — but in Singapore it happened in about a decade rather than a century, as Britain's empire was ending after the Second World War.",
      },
      body:
        "| Date | Step | Change in representation |\n|---|---|---|\n| 20 Mar 1948 | First elections, to the colonial **Legislative Council** | Only **6 seats** were elected — officials and nominated members were the majority. Only British subjects who registered could vote; about 23,000 did. Women could vote from the start |\n| 2 Apr 1955 | Election under the **Rendel Constitution** | 25 of the 32 seats in a new Legislative Assembly elected; automatic registration of voters. David Marshall became Chief Minister. Britain kept control of defence, foreign affairs and internal security |\n| 30 May 1959 | Election for **full internal self-government** | All 51 seats elected; voting compulsory. Lee Kuan Yew became the first Prime Minister. Britain still controlled defence and foreign affairs |\n| 16 Sep 1963 | **Merger**: Singapore joins Malaysia | Singapore kept its own elected Assembly and sent 15 members to the Malaysian Parliament |\n| 9 Aug 1965 | **Independence** | Singapore becomes a sovereign, independent republic |\n\n**Parliamentary democracy today.** Singapore kept the Westminster-style system it inherited from Britain: MPs are elected in constituencies, and the party with a majority in Parliament forms the government. Distinctive features include:\n\n- **Compulsory voting** (since 1959): a citizen who does not vote is removed from the register until they apply to be restored.\n- **Voting age 21** (Britain lowered its voting age to 18 in 1969).\n- **Non-Constituency MPs (NCMPs)**, since 1984: seats offered to the best-performing losing opposition candidates, so that Parliament always includes some opposition members.\n- **Group Representation Constituencies (GRCs)**, since 1988: teams of MPs elected together, at least one of whom must be from a minority community (Malay, Indian or another minority), to guarantee multiracial representation.\n- **An elected President**, created by a 1991 change to the Constitution; the first presidential election was held in 1993. The President has custodial powers, for example over the use of the national reserves.\n\nLike all electoral systems, these features are debated. A historian's job is to describe the changes accurately, explain the reasons given for them, and weigh the evidence.",
